WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.700
In 2006, Jennifer Carpenter was an up-and-coming
actress whose luck changed virtually overnight

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.780
when she booked the second lead on Showtime's
breakout serial killer drama Dexter.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.820
Carpenter frequently stole the spotlight from
star Michael C. Hall with her impassioned

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.470
turn as Dexter's foster sister Debra, a homicide
detective whose caseload often overlapped

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:20.380
with her duplicitous brother's own crimes.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.420
It's been almost seven years since Dexter's
divisive finale episode aired, and fans are

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.860
still wondering if Carpenter's Debra got the
send-off she deserved.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.240
As for Carpenter herself, she's maintained
a lower profile recently, but she's more than

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.980
kept herself busy since leaving Debra and
Dexter behind.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.320
Like so many actors in and around Hollywood
over the past decade or so, Jennifer Carpenter

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:43.690
eventually found herself with a ticket to
ride the Marvel money train, but it wasn't

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.060
quite of the first-class nature.

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:49.000
She only got to contribute her voice to the
ever-expanding Marvel-verse through the animated

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.510
project Avengers Confidential: Black Widow
&amp; Punisher.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:57.679
In the 2014 anime film, it's Carpenter's golden
pipes - and not Scarlett Johansson's - you

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.080
hear as tough-as-nails Natasha Romanoff.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:02.460
"Evening, Castle.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.900
What have you got there?

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.060
Friend of yours?"

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.380
Marvel's Black Widow isn't the only big-time
comic book character Jennifer Carpenter has

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.860
voiced since her days on Dexter.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.200
Her second turn in the animated comic book
movie world found her voicing Selina Kyle,

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:22.689
best known as Gotham City's Catwoman, in Warner
Bros. and DC Comics' lavish, 19th-century

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:27.130
reimagining of the Dark Knight saga, Batman:
Gotham by Gaslight.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:32.609
Released to largely positive reviews in 2018,
Gotham by Gaslight was adapted from the 1989

00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:37.030
graphic novel of the same name, itself the
first official offering from DC's "Elseworlds"

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:37.540
imprint.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.220
Both the book and the film feature Bruce Wayne
defending the streets of a gloriously steam-punky

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:47.320
Gotham as a Victorian-era Batman, eventually
facing off against the infamous killer Jack

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:47.820
the Ripper.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.480
But Carpenter hasn't shied away from performing
in front of the camera as well.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.700
Since leaving Dexter behind, the actress has
sought out similar success on the small screen.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.759
That search ultimately led the actress to
the hyper-competitive realm of network television,

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:02.690
where she's delivered strong work in largely
forgettable dramas.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:07.259
The first of Carpenter's post-Dexter TV roles
came on the short-lived dramedy Limitless,

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:10.129
which was based on the Bradley Cooper-starring
movie of the same name.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:13.760
That flick found Cooper tripping the life
fantastic after ingesting a wonder drug that

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.220
enabled him to utilize the full potential
of his brain.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.040
The television adaptation, which was produced
by Cooper and Joker director Todd Phillips,

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.110
followed a similar set up.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.480
This time, however, the "gifted" man is out
to make the world a safer place, and Carpenter

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.570
portrays the FBI Agent tasked with helping
focus the man's energies on solving crimes.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.960
"You will have plenty of stuff to go through
in the morning."

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.160
Carpenter's next television venture found
her playing the other side of the law as a

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.380
traitorous former CIA Agent forced to team
up with the FBI to help track down their most-wanted

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:45.880
spy.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.380
Titled The Enemy Within, Carpenter's timely
espionage drama had the look of a winner when

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:55.140
it hit the air in 2019, but tragically never
found its audience and was cancelled after

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.740
airing just 13 episodes.

00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:00.439
The actress seems to have had better luck
on the big screen, having found a patron of

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:04.799
sorts in cinematic rabble-rouser S. Craig
Zahler, whose modern B-movie marvels include

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:11.050
Brawl in Cell Block 99 and 2019's disturbing
dirty cop caper Dragged Across Concrete.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:15.879
In the hyper-violent Brawl in Cell Block 99,
Carpenter plays Lauren Thomas, the wife of

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:20.750
Vince Vaughn's Bradley Thomas, a retired boxer
whose life of crime lands him behind bars

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.160
at the Redleaf Correctional Facility, where
his troubles only grow more intense.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.769
Throughout it all, Carpenter stayed devoted
to the madness while breathing necessary life

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:31.269
into a character that's not quite three-dimensional.

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:35.269
She did the same in Dragged Across Concrete,
bringing a refreshing humanity to tortured

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:39.560
young mother Kelly Summer, who serves as a
smaller character against leads Mel Gibson

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.470
and Vince Vaughn, who play a pair of cops
suspended after assaulting a suspect.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:49.129
In late 2019, Carpenter was cast in the film
A Mouthful of Air, alongside Amanda Seyfried

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:50.319
and Britt Robertson.

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:54.390
The film is an adaptation of Amy Koppelman's
novel of the same name, and centers on a new

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.569
mother and children's book author who uses
her own imaginary world as a means of escape

00:03:58.569 --> 00:03:59.940
from postpartum depression.

